{"parentUuid":"d7947d9f-ad1c-4920-93ea-5e3f17c44812","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01DufkoeNVwaimg4Nu2fdVZj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DUbfNeVgMuzYYVYLaeHyhn","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"max empty rectangle\" optimization grid placement large scale 80x80 anytime"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1331,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5830,"cache_read_input_tokens":21308,"output_tokens":382,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5830},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1331,"output_tokens":382,"cache_read_input_tokens":21308,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5830,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5830,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorbcBvxuLrRm1Vnd3Ln","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"203fc4c6-acf4-49b5-ab04-284e76fa1678","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:53.944Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"84d2818a-1f05-4a3b-a57c-9b121833b503","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CsuVaYDmvfAKDK79VEyBvx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DRAT LRAT proof certificate SAT solver MIP optimization\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GRAT -- Efficient Formally Verified SAT Solver Certification Tool Chain\",\"url\":\"https://www21.in.tum.de/~lammich/grat/\"},{\"title\":\"Efficient Verified (UN)SAT Certificate Checking | Journal of Automated Reasoning | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10817-019-09525-z\"},{\"title\":\"A Flexible Proof Format for SAT Solver-Elaborator ...\",\"url\":\"https://lmcs.episciences.org/9357/pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Efficient Verified (UN)SAT Certificate Checking\",\"url\":\"https://d-nb.info/1195523297/34\"},{\"title\":\"Efficient Proof Checking with LRAT in CADICAL (Work in Progress)\",\"url\":\"https://cca.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/papers/PollittFleuryBiere-MBMV23.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - marijnheule/drat-trim: The DRAT-trim proof checker\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/marijnheule/drat-trim\"},{\"title\":\"Certifying MIP-Based Presolve Reductions for 0–1 Integer Linear Programs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.09277\"},{\"title\":\"[1907.00087] DRAT-based Bit-Vector Proofs in CVC4This work was supported in part by DARPA (N66001-18-C-4012 and FA8650-18-2-7861) and NSF (1814369).\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1907.00087\"},{\"title\":\"Efficient Certified RAT Verification⋆ - UT Computer Science\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~marijn/publications/lrat.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The GRAT Tool Chain | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-66263-3_29\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I can provide you with information about DRAT, LRAT, proof certificates, and SAT solvers:\n\n## Key Proof Formats for SAT Solvers\n\nThe DRAT-trim proof checker can be used to check whether a propositional formula in the DIMACS format is unsatisfiable by validating that the proof is a certificate of unsatisfiability of the formula. The DRAT format is designed based on the goals that: it should be easy to emit clausal proofs, proofs should be compact, proof validation should be efficient, and all techniques used in state-of-the-art SAT solvers should be expressible in the format.\n\nLRAT is a new format that extends the DRAT format with hints that facilitate a simple and fast validation algorithm. The LRAT proof format provides more detailed information: Each clause gets an identifier and each step is the result of resolving several clauses together, with the list of clauses given as justification for each step.\n\n## Performance Comparisons\n\nThe proof format DRAT used in the SAT competition is rather inefficient to check, often even slower to check than it takes the SAT solver to solve the instance and generate the proof, while LRAT on the one hand is much easier and way more efficient to check, but on the other hand much harder to generate.\n\nGRAT is actually faster than the unverified state-of-the-art tool drat-trim, and significantly faster than the verified LRAT tool.\n\n## Connection to MIP Optimization\n\nA number of proof formats have been developed, such as DRAT, GRIT, and LRAT, which are used to certify the whole solving process including preprocessing. The SAT community has dealt with correctness problems by requiring that solvers produce machine-verifiable certificates of correctness for their computations, with proof formats like DRAT, GRIT, and LRAT used to certify the whole solving process including preprocessing.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"228f0f59-efc2-4748-97e3-1a9967f3b6f8","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:59.756Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"84d2818a-1f05-4a3b-a57c-9b121833b503","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"d7947d9f-ad1c-4920-93ea-5e3f17c44812","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EgATj16jyUd2ea9gfBb3Ad","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Google CP-SAT industrial case study termination criteria gap tolerance production\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Optimality Tolerance in CP-SAT/SCIP\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/Yra_AIJ3kkE\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Coding Patterns - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/06_coding_patterns.html\"},{\"title\":\"Primal Integral CP-SAT\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/Gqt4xwEHTUg\"},{\"title\":\"Basic Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver returning non-optimal solutions with status \\\"OPTIMAL\\\" · Issue #3639 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3639\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about Google CP-SAT's termination criteria and gap tolerance settings, though the results don't contain a specific industrial case study with that exact name.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Termination Criteria and Gap Tolerance in CP-SAT:**\n\nIn addition to limiting runtime, you can specify acceptable solution quality using absolute_gap_limit and relative_gap_limit. The absolute limit stops the solver when the solution is within a specified value of the bound, and the relative limit stops the solver when the objective value is within a specified ratio of the bound.\n\nSolver parameters include max_time_in_seconds for time limit and relative_gap_limit for optimality tolerance.\n\n**Gap Definition:**\n\nThe gap is between the best bound and the best solution, with the gap always positive and decreasing, reaching 0 at optimal.\n\n**Production/Optimization Configuration:**\n\nTo stop when the solution is (provably) within 5% of the optimum, specific relative gap limits can be configured.\n\nThe search results provide documentation about CP-SAT's termination mechanisms but do not contain a specific published industrial case study with production optimization using these termination criteria. If you're looking for a specific case study document, you might want to search Google's OR-Tools documentation or research papers on CP-SAT applications more directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8779e258-d9fd-4008-bb27-be62d57bf401","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:59.877Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d7947d9f-ad1c-4920-93ea-5e3f17c44812","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"20a89a54-4591-4c91-b0c0-5cc7d80d0df6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Uw8kZm1d2Q4mVHQcwQmTc7","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"VeriPB pseudo-Boolean proof certificate CP-SAT optimization VeriPB 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Pseudo Boolean Competition 2025\",\"url\":\"http://www.cril.univ-artois.fr/PB25/\"},{\"title\":\"Certifying Parity Reasoning Eﬃciently Using Pseudo-Boolean Proofs∗\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.12185\"},{\"title\":\"PBLean: Pseudo-Boolean Proof Certificates for Lean 4\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08692v2\"},{\"title\":\"Practically Feasible Proof Logging for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.21\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - StephanGocht/VeriPB: Verifier for pseudo-Boolean proofs · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/StephanGocht/VeriPB\"},{\"title\":\"Documentation of VeriPB and CakePB for the SAT Competition 2025 Markus Anders\",\"url\":\"https://satcompetition.github.io/2025/downloads/checkers/veripb.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Certified CNF Translations for Pseudo-Boolean Solving (Extended Abstract)∗\",\"url\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2023/0716.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Pseudo-Boolean Proof Logging for Optimal Classical Planning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2504.18443\"},{\"title\":\"Pseudo-Boolean Reasoning About States and Transitions to Certify Dynamic Programming and Decision Diagram Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2024.9\"},{\"title\":\"A Multi-Stage Proof Logging Framework to Certify the Correctness of CP Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol307-cp2024/LIPIcs.CP.2024.11/LIPIcs.CP.2024.11.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about VeriPB, pseudo-Boolean proof certificates, and optimization. Here are the key findings:\n\n## VeriPB and Pseudo-Boolean Proofs\n\nVeriPB is a tool for verifying refutations and solutions written in Python and C++ that focuses on linear pseudo-Boolean proofs utilizing cutting planes reasoning. The VeriPB proof format provides two modes: an augmented mode with high-level rules (symmetry breaking, redundance-based strengthening) and a kernel mode (version 3.0) with only primitive operations, where the kernel format's small rule set of just eight rule types enables a rigorous soundness proof.\n\n## 2025 Competition and Recent Developments\n\nFour tracks are open unconditionally: PBS (PB Satisfaction) and PBO (PB Optimization) for linear constraints, with and without certificates of unsatisfiability/optimality, where certificates must be generated in the VeriPB format.\n\nA recent efficient toolchain based on VeriPB and CakePB was presented for formally verified pseudo-Boolean optimization. This toolchain implements proof logging for techniques in state-of-the-art solvers including core-guided search and linear programming integration, with experimental evaluation showing proof logging and checking performance is now quite close to SAT solving levels and practically feasible.\n\n## CP-SAT Integration\n\nConstraints can be encoded as big-M constraints in the pseudo-Boolean model for integration with constraint programming solvers.\n\nThe search results show active development in pseudo-Boolean proof certification for optimization problems in 2025, with improvements in practical feasibility for real-world applications.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"14a3b416-1cef-4e69-9aa2-7f9efe288d30","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:00.106Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"20a89a54-4591-4c91-b0c0-5cc7d80d0df6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"203fc4c6-acf4-49b5-ab04-284e76fa1678","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DUbfNeVgMuzYYVYLaeHyhn","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"max empty rectangle\" optimization grid placement large scale 80x80 anytime\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Largest empty rectangle - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Largest_empty_rectangle\"},{\"title\":\"maxempty: Maximally Empty Rectangle Problem in adagio: Discrete and Global Optimization Routines\",\"url\":\"https://rdrr.io/rforge/adagio/man/maxempty.html\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal Rectangle Packing: An Absolute Placement ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1402.0557\"},{\"title\":\"The Maximal Rectangle Problem | Dr Dobb's\",\"url\":\"https://www.drdobbs.com/database/the-maximal-rectangle-problem/184410529\"},{\"title\":\"Fast parallel algorithms for the Maximum Empty Rectangle ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.ias.ac.in/article/fulltext/sadh/017/01/0221-0236\"},{\"title\":\"85. Maximal Rectangle - In-Depth Explanation\",\"url\":\"https://algo.monster/liteproblems/85\"},{\"title\":\"A Complete Guide to CSS Grid Layout | CSS-Tricks\",\"url\":\"https://css-tricks.com/snippets/css/complete-guide-grid/\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Computing the Largest Empty Rectangle\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/225786909_Computing_the_Largest_Empty_Rectangle\"},{\"title\":\"Fast algorithms for computing the largest empty rectangle | Proceedings of the third annual symposium on Computational geometry\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/41958.41988\"},{\"title\":\"A Deep Dive Into CSS Grid minmax()\",\"url\":\"https://ishadeed.com/article/css-grid-minmax/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about the maximum empty rectangle problem and related optimization approaches:\n\n## Maximum Empty Rectangle Problem\n\nThe problem involves finding the largest-area rectangle with sides parallel to a bounding rectangle that lies within the bounding rectangle and does not contain any of the given points.\n\n## Algorithm Approaches\n\nThere are several algorithmic approaches for solving this problem at different computational complexities:\n\n1. **Classic Algorithms**: One algorithm takes O(n log³ n) time and O(n) memory space, while a more complicated algorithm takes O(n log² n) time and O(n) memory space.\n\n2. **Enumeration Methods**: Several algorithms solve this problem by enumerating all candidate empty rectangles with expected and worst case running times of O(n log n) and O(n²), respectively, using O(n) space.\n\n3. **Heuristic Approaches**: For non-convex polygon cases, heuristic algorithms like PSO (Particle Swarm Optimization) and Tabu Search can be applied to find the maximum area of the rectangle. These approximation algorithms have an O(n³) time complexity and O(n²) space complexity for PSO, and O(n⁴) time complexity and O(n²) space complexity for Tabu Search.\n\n## Implementation Tools\n\nThe `maxempty` function in R's adagio package provides an implementation for finding maximally empty rectangles.\n\nFor your specific case involving an 80x80 grid, these algorithms and approaches can be applied, with the choice depending on the number of obstacles/points and your computational constraints.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"34cf8cb0-eb7d-4561-825e-dd0050b51185","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:00.791Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"203fc4c6-acf4-49b5-ab04-284e76fa1678","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"47509aa7-834b-48e6-8e85-5be51645de66","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FTCiiz5qzJ1R19rCXEbzpt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"chip placement floorplanning anytime solver gap tolerance industrial deployment\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"what is Floorplanning - VLSI- Physical Design For Freshers\",\"url\":\"https://www.physicaldesign4u.com/2019/12/floorplanning-floor-planning-is-art-of.html\"},{\"title\":\"A graph placement methodology for fast chip design | Nature\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03544-w\"},{\"title\":\"How Chip Floorplan Design Automation Accelerates Chip Design | Synopsys Blog\",\"url\":\"https://www.synopsys.com/blogs/chip-design/chip-floorplan-design-automation.html\"},{\"title\":\"BENCHMARKING END-TO-END PERFORMANCE OF AI-BASED CHIP PLACEMENT ALGORITHMS\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.15026\"},{\"title\":\"Floorplan – SignOff Semiconductors\",\"url\":\"https://signoffsemiconductors.com/floorplan/\"},{\"title\":\"Floorplanning - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/floorplanning\"},{\"title\":\"GraphPlanner: Floorplanning with Graph Neural Network | ACM Transactions on Design Automation of Electronic Systems\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3555804\"},{\"title\":\"Multi-objective Floorplanning optimization engaging dynamic programming for system on chip - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0026269223002550\"},{\"title\":\"Floor Planning | SoC Labs\",\"url\":\"https://soclabs.org/design-flow/floor-planning\"},{\"title\":\"Floorplan (microelectronics) - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floorplan_(microelectronics)\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about chip placement, floorplanning, anytime solvers, gap tolerance, and industrial deployment. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview of Chip Floorplanning and Placement\n\nChip floorplanning is the engineering task of designing the physical layout of a computer chip, and despite five decades of research, it has defied automation, requiring months of intense effort by physical design engineers to produce manufacturable layouts.\n\n## Industrial Deployment and Challenges\n\nFloorplan design is time-consuming and tedious, with emerging verticals such as artificial intelligence, high-performance computing, and hyperscale data centers leading to increased chip design complexity and unique architectural challenges. With growing design size and complexity, the number of macros in a design is also rapidly increasing, and as a result, floorplanning design is dominating project schedules.\n\n## Optimization Approaches and Solvers\n\nStandard cell placement phase typically utilizes analytical solvers to secure an optimized configuration that not only minimizes wirelength but also enhances the electrical and timing performance of the chip.\n\nSimulated annealing is a widely used method for floorplanning optimization, offering the advantage of escaping local optima by allowing moves to higher-cost solutions with a non-zero probability. The simulated annealing process for floorplanning relies on four basic components: solution space, neighborhood structure, cost function, and annealing schedule.\n\n## Gap Between Academic and Industrial Approaches\n\nDue to the oversimplification of datasets in early years, there exists a significant gap between these datasets and real-world applications. The usually used Bookshelf format is overly simplified so that placement results given in such format are inapplicable for subsequent stages to obtain a valid final design, which significantly limits the use of existing AI-based placement algorithms in practical industrial scenarios.\n\nThe search results reveal that while various optimization approaches exist for chip placement and floorplanning, there remains a substantial challenge in bridging the gap between theoretical approaches and practical industrial deployment requirements.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0aca7264-2e22-4562-9cf5-091403ca7743","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:01.700Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"47509aa7-834b-48e6-8e85-5be51645de66","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
