{"parentUuid":"70cd00ca-1173-4c2a-b227-a5e0d660d847","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01FNHnkfxTrpms6z3Dy6UpuW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Sc4CahBZ3LKBGQDF24GFj6","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"campaign solver checkpoint resume gap improvement anytime iterate epsilon shrink"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7548,"cache_read_input_tokens":37294,"output_tokens":310,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7548},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":310,"cache_read_input_tokens":37294,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7548,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7548,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caorey3tBYqqFzWdXveon","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"232cc597-4c1d-41ca-88a1-05fa60154762","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:38.560Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"166c7a68-6632-4d4a-b6af-78aa749375e8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018VmhFwCKT9m4aJSuGehMQE","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT relative_gap_limit FEASIBLE returned status real behavior github issue\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT, UNKNOWN status with complete solution hint · Issue #3750 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3750\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Log - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/understanding_the_log.html\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"order of constraints changes cp-sat outcome from feasible to infeasible · Issue #4168 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4168\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver returning non-optimal solutions with status \\\"OPTIMAL\\\" · Issue #3639 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3639\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT 9.7.2996 Sometimes feasible, sometimes infeasible · Issue #3938 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3938\"},{\"title\":\"Coding Patterns - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/06_coding_patterns.html\"},{\"title\":\"SCIP Solver returns solution as OPTIMAL when it isn't · Issue #3672 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3672\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver couldn't prove the solution is optimal if num_workers < 5 but otherwise it did instantly. · Issue #3662 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3662\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found several relevant GitHub issues related to CP-SAT and solution status behavior:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Related Issue #3750** - This issue discusses using solution hints as warm start where \"The solution hint is complete and is feasible\" but the solver stopped by time limit in presolve and returned status UNKNOWN. The case shows parameters with `relative_gap_limit: 0.1`.\n\n**Status Documentation** - CP-SAT response status can be FEASIBLE, OPTIMAL, INFEASIBLE, UNKNOWN, or MODEL_INVALID.\n\n**Gap Limit Behavior** - If the gap is reached, the search status will be OPTIMAL, but one can check the best objective bound to see the actual gap. This is important context for `relative_gap_limit` parameter behavior.\n\n**Related Issue #3662** - In one case with num_workers=4, the solver spent 60 seconds and only returned a FEASIBLE solution rather than OPTIMAL, even though both found the same objective value - the solver failed to prove optimality with lower worker counts.\n\nThe search results show various cases where CP-SAT returns FEASIBLE status instead of the expected OPTIMAL when parameters like `relative_gap_limit` are set, particularly around gap-based termination and proof of optimality behavior. There are also issues related to race conditions and worker count affecting solution status accuracy.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"12f2c2b5-e552-42a8-bb0e-6f53cc2c894e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:43.671Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"166c7a68-6632-4d4a-b6af-78aa749375e8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"e61ef45d-c497-4161-9f97-d20a1bc918b3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012XSvLwG6Fobu3xwgi3wKK9","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition anytime gap certificate placement industrial\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Logic-based Benders decomposition with a partial assignment acceleration technique for avionics scheduling - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305054822001769\"},{\"title\":\"Planning and Scheduling by Logic-Based Benders Decomposition | Operations Research\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/opre.1060.0371\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-based Benders decomposition for planning and scheduling: a computational analysis | The Knowledge Engineering Review | Cambridge Core\",\"url\":\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/knowledge-engineering-review/article/abs/logicbased-benders-decomposition-for-planning-and-scheduling-a-computational-analysis/70EE77BFAE516C56F3363A90A3786D84\"},{\"title\":\"Critical-Path-Search Logic-Based Benders Decomposition Approaches for Flexible Job Shop Scheduling | INFORMS Journal on Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/ijoo.2021.0056\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-based Benders Decomposition for Large-scale Optimization J. N. Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/LBBDbookChapter.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition for Integrated Process Configuration and Production Planning Problems | INFORMS Journal on Computing\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/abs/10.1287/ijoc.2021.1079\"},{\"title\":\"Combining optimisation and simulation using logic-based Benders decomposition - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S037722172300591X\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/386592005_Logic-Based_Benders_Decomposition\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-based Benders decomposition for bi-objective parallel machine selection and job scheduling with release dates and resource consumption - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305054823003921\"},{\"title\":\"Enhanced Logic-Based Benders Decomposition Approaches for Flexible Job Shop Scheduling Benchmark Problem with Discrete Operation Sequence Flexibility - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S100093452600074X\"}]\n\nI've performed a web search for \"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition anytime gap certificate placement industrial\" and found several relevant results.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nLogic-based Benders decomposition (LBBD) has improved the state of the art for solving a variety of planning and scheduling problems, in part by combining the complementary strengths of constraint programming and mixed integer programming (MIP).\n\nLogic-based Benders decomposition (LBBD) is a substantial generalization of classical Benders decomposition that, in principle, allows the subproblem to be any optimization problem rather than specifically a linear or nonlinear programming problem.\n\nThe search results show that LBBD has been widely applied to industrial optimization problems, including:\n- Planning and scheduling problems where tasks are allocated to facilities using MILP and scheduled using CP, linked via logic-based Benders decomposition.\n- Flexible job shop scheduling problems (F-JSSPs), using an efficient CP–logic-based Benders decomposition (CP-LBBD) technique that combines the complementary strengths of MIP and CP models.\n- Production planning problems with process configuration decisions.\n\nRegarding performance metrics like gap certificates and anytime algorithms, performance profiles indicate the proportion of instances for which a given gap was obtained, and methods were able to solve the majority of instances to optimality within specified time limits, achieving a small optimality gap for the rest.\n\nThe results demonstrate that LBBD is particularly effective for industrial applications where problems have decomposable structure combining discrete assignment/configuration decisions with scheduling or sequencing subproblems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9bbfe992-bf54-417c-bf99-e68381b986d7","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:44.796Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e61ef45d-c497-4161-9f97-d20a1bc918b3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"232cc597-4c1d-41ca-88a1-05fa60154762","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sc4CahBZ3LKBGQDF24GFj6","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"campaign solver checkpoint resume gap improvement anytime iterate epsilon shrink\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Optimal Checkpointing for Adjoint Multistage Time-Stepping Schemes\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.13879\"},{\"title\":\"Resuming from checkpoint with DQN and epsilon greedy let timesteps start from 0 again - RLlib - Ray\",\"url\":\"https://discuss.ray.io/t/resuming-from-checkpoint-with-dqn-and-epsilon-greedy-let-timesteps-start-from-0-again/7439\"},{\"title\":\"If I load from a checkpoint, does it iterate again over all data? - nlp - PyTorch Forums\",\"url\":\"https://discuss.pytorch.org/t/if-i-load-from-a-checkpoint-does-it-iterate-again-over-all-data/117231\"},{\"title\":\"The Resume Gap Strategy: When to Explain, When to Restructure, When to Say Nothing — Optim Careers\",\"url\":\"https://optimcareers.com/expert-articles/the-resume-gap-strategy\"},{\"title\":\"Shrink or Substitute: Handling Process Failures in\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.04523\"},{\"title\":\"Shrink or Substitute: Handling Process Failures in HPC ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1454399\"},{\"title\":\"Checkpoint Policy Optimization / Review - Check Point CheckMates\",\"url\":\"https://community.checkpoint.com/t5/Firewall-and-Security-Management/Checkpoint-Policy-Optimization-Review/td-p/159555\"},{\"title\":\"ms-swift/docs/source_en/Instruction/Command-line-parameters.md at main · modelscope/ms-swift\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/modelscope/ms-swift/blob/main/docs/source_en/Instruction/Command-line-parameters.md\"},{\"title\":\"TechTalk: Security Gateway Performance Optimizatio... - Check Point CheckMates\",\"url\":\"https://community.checkpoint.com/t5/General-Topics/TechTalk-Security-Gateway-Performance-Optimization-with-Tim-Hall/td-p/29833\"},{\"title\":\"Performance Eﬃcient Multiresilience using\",\"url\":\"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1493144\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, the query appears to relate to technical computing and scientific computing topics. Here's what I found:\n\nThe search results include several academic papers and technical discussions related to checkpointing and solver optimization strategies in high-performance computing:\n\n1. **Checkpointing in Adjoint Solvers**: The CAMS algorithm describes how checkpoints can be used in adjoint ODE solvers, determining whether a checkpoint should be stored at the end of a time step during forward runs or recomputation stages.\n\n2. **Shrink vs. Substitute Recovery Strategies**: The shrink strategy for process failure recovery eliminates the need for spare processes allocated at design time, and arbitrary numbers of process failures can be sustained as long as there are enough surviving processes to continue execution.\n\n3. **Multiresilience and Error Detection**: Checkpoints can be utilized for soft rollback when errors are detected, and to minimize recomputations, soft error detections should be performed regularly so rollback happens quickly.\n\n4. **Solver Iteration and Epsilon Parameters**: The search results also reference machine learning frameworks with epsilon parameters and checkpoint resumption capabilities used in training algorithms.\n\nThe combination of terms in your query (checkpoint, resume, gap improvement, anytime, iterate, epsilon, shrink) suggests research into iterative solver optimization, checkpointing strategies, and fault recovery mechanisms in computational science and machine learning applications.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"42646ecf-1627-415a-aaed-89c53b6190b5","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:45.210Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"232cc597-4c1d-41ca-88a1-05fa60154762","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"70cd00ca-1173-4c2a-b227-a5e0d660d847","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QWwvKgUY9tnzfWsqRt5G73","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"game design level layout optimization player perception \"1%\" optimal vs solving time\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Optimization | The Level Design Book\",\"url\":\"https://book.leveldesignbook.com/process/env-art/optimization\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing Game Level Design Techniques | PDF | Perception | Distance\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/579085791/Level-Design-In-pursuit-of-better-levels\"},{\"title\":\"17 Things You Can Do to Improve Your Game Level Design\",\"url\":\"https://retrostylegames.com/blog/17-things-improve-your-game-level-design/\"},{\"title\":\"Level (video games) - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Level_(video_games)\"},{\"title\":\"7 Best Practices for Effective Video Game Level Design\",\"url\":\"https://pixelfield.co.uk/blog/level-design-in-video-games-7-best-practices/\"},{\"title\":\"Decision Modeling and Optimization in Game Design, Part 1: Introduction\",\"url\":\"https://www.gamedeveloper.com/design/decision-modeling-and-optimization-in-game-design-part-1-introduction\"},{\"title\":\"How Not to Ruin A Player's Gaming Experience: The Do's and Don'ts of Video Game Level Design\",\"url\":\"https://gamedesigning.org/learn/level-design/\"},{\"title\":\"Metrics | The Level Design Book\",\"url\":\"https://book.leveldesignbook.com/process/blockout/metrics\"},{\"title\":\"Game Level Design: Tips, Process, and Best Practices\",\"url\":\"https://300mind.studio/blog/game-level-design-guide/\"},{\"title\":\"Mobile Game Level Design: Tips and Best Practices | BamBamTastic Games, LLC\",\"url\":\"https://bambamtastic.com/mobile-game-level-design/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about game level design optimization and player perception, though the specific \"1%\" optimal concept you mentioned doesn't appear in these results. Here's what I found:\n\n## Level Layout Optimization and Player Perception\n\nWhile humans have good depth perception and sense of scale, it doesn't directly translate 1:1 when it comes to games. This is a critical consideration for level designers, as if the player doesn't get a good sense for scale and depth it can result in missed jumps, confusion, etc.\n\n## Design Optimization Approaches\n\nNever try to optimize on faith or a hunch, because you could be wrong about what's making the game run slowly. Profiling should be the first step of any optimization pass!\n\nMore broadly, most of game design is a process of search. When we design, we are evaluating many different possible design configurations to solve a given design problem, whether it be the way the rooms in a dungeon are connected, the set of features and capabilities that different types of game agents will possess, the specific \"magic numbers\" that govern unit effectiveness in a combat system, or even the combination of features our game will include in the first place.\n\n## Solving Time vs. Optimization Trade-offs\n\nInterestingly, many metrics are just \"good numbers\" and guidelines you give yourself, they're not a scientific infallible way to perfect level design. Metrics are useful, but metrics are not magic.\n\nThe search results don't contain specific information about the \"1% optimal\" concept or direct comparisons between optimal solutions and solving time trade-offs in level design. If you're looking for more specific academic or technical resources on this topic, a more specialized search might be needed.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"bd1fbb77-3994-42e7-b4ec-2105694cd39b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:46.413Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"70cd00ca-1173-4c2a-b227-a5e0d660d847","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bd1fbb77-3994-42e7-b4ec-2105694cd39b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a6480e76a7177e6fd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_0135sQR3PbHYhU2M44PXByqz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"我已经收集了足够信息。现在直接整理输出。\n\n---\n\n# CP-SAT Anytime + ε-Optimal + Bound Certificate 调研报告\n\n## 1. CP-SAT 的 anytime 模式与 gap limit 参数\n\nCP-SAT 原生支持 anytime：`solver.parameters.absolute_gap_limit`（绝对差）和 `relative_gap_limit`（相对比例）任意一个达成都会停。关键定义：absolute = `abs(O - B)`，relative = `abs(O - B) / max(1, abs(O))`，B 是 `BestObjectiveBound`，O 是 `ObjectiveValue`。**重要陷阱：gap 达成时 status 返回 `OPTIMAL` 而不是 `FEASIBLE`**——必须自己读 `solver.BestObjectiveBound()` 才能知道真实 gap。这意味着我们 PROJECT_LOCK 要靠 `status == OPTIMAL` 判定\"证明最优\"是不安全的。Certificate 形态：CP-SAT 不内置 LRAT/DRAT 输出，只能通过 log + `(ObjectiveValue, BestObjectiveBound, gap, wall_time)` 四元组自构造审计记录。\n\n## 2. MIP 世界的 ε-optimal 工业实践\n\nGurobi `MIPGap` 默认 `0.0001`（0.01%），但工业部署里 1%~5% 是常态。Gurobi 官方语录：\"a good answer now is often better than a perfect answer tomorrow\"。供应链/排程类问题通常 1%，VRP 经常 5%，能源调度（economic dispatch）能接受 2% 换 1000x 加速（[arxiv 2510.15850](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.15850)）。Gap certificate 的工业格式很简单：log 记录 `(best_obj, best_bound, gap%, time, gap_limit_hit)`，外加一句 \"solver terminated with relative gap ≤ X% as certified by dual bound B\"。审计场景下 Gurobi 给的 `MIPGap` attribute 就当 certificate 用。\n\n## 3. 学术：bounded suboptimality + proof certificate\n\n最前沿是 **VeriPB + CakePB**（[CP 2025 论文](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/html/LIPIcs.CP.2025.21/LIPIcs.CP.2025.21.html)），首个达到 SAT proof logging 性能水平的 PB 优化证明工具链，覆盖 core-guided search、LP 集成（Farkas certificates）、cut generation。CP 求解器侧 [CP 2024 multi-stage proof logging framework](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol307-cp2024/LIPIcs.CP.2024.11/LIPIcs.CP.2024.11.pdf) 给出了通用框架。但 **CP-SAT 本身没有 VeriPB 输出**，要走这条路得把模型先转 PB。对 placement 问题：可以把 ghost rectangle 编成 PB 约束输出 VeriPB 证明，但工程量巨大。\n\n## 4. \"certified_exact\" 术语澄清\n\n学术界 \"certified\" 严格指**机器可验证的形式化证明**（VeriPB/LRAT），而工业界 \"certified optimal\" 通常指 **solver 报告 OPTIMAL 状态 + dual bound 匹配**——后者更宽松，等价于 `MIPGap = 0`。我们 PROJECT_LOCK 里的 `certified_exact` 没有形式化定义，按当前代码语义是\"OR-Tools 返回 OPTIMAL 且 ghost rect 通过 binding/routing/flow 全链路\"。**业界类似项目**（chip floorplanning、batch economic dispatch）几乎都接受 1-5% gap，纯 OPTIMAL 极少见。所以我们的 lock 标准其实比工业基线还严。\n\n## 5. anytime 的\"递增证明\"\n\nCP-SAT 的 `solution_callback.on_solution_callback` 每发现更优解触发，可访问 `self.objective_value` + `self.best_objective_bound`，从而构造 **gap 时间序列**：`[(t1, obj1, bound1), (t2, obj2, bound2), ...]`，这是天然的 anytime certificate。对 168h 长跑：每隔 N 分钟把 `(best_obj, best_bound, gap)` 写到 checkpoint，168h 结束时取最后那一条作为最终 certificate。campaign 框架已有 checkpoint 基础设施，加这个增量字段是 1-2 天工作量。\n\n## 6. ε-optimality 在我们项目的可行性\n\n**业务层面**：玩家用户对 70x70 grid 上\"最大空白矩形\"的差异感知阈值很高——area 差 1%（约 49 格里差 0.5 格 × 0.5 格，肉眼几乎无差）vs 等几个小时对玩家是绝对赢。差 5% 时 min_side 可能少 1 格（6→5），这才是真正分界点。**建议两档 gap**：area 接受 1%，min_side 必须严格证（min_side 是离散整数，gap 没意义）。**PROJECT_LOCK 改写代价**：要把 `certified_exact` 拆成 `area_eps_certified` + `min_side_strict`，spec/test/lock 三处联动改，约 2-3 天。\n\n## 7. 业界 anytime 案例\n\n最有代表性是上面提到的 [Self-Certifying Primal-Dual Optimization Proxies](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.15850)：电网调度从\"必须全局最优\"放到\"2% gap\"换来 1000x 加速。Gurobi 官方 [supply chain industry 页面](https://www.gurobi.com/industry/optimization-for-the-supply-chain-industry/) 也明确把\"near-optimal in time\"作为产品价值主张。\n\n## 8. ObjectiveBound 接口具体用法\n\n```python\nclass GapCertCallback(cp_model.CpSolverSolutionCallback):\n    def on_solution_callback(self):\n        obj = self.objective_value\n        bound = self.best_objective_bound\n        gap = abs(obj - bound) / max(1.0, abs(obj))\n        # 写 checkpoint\n        if gap < target_eps:\n            self.stop_search()\n\nsolver.parameters.relative_gap_limit = 0.01  # 1%\nsolver.parameters.max_time_in_seconds = 168 * 3600\nstatus = solver.solve(model, GapCertCallback())\nfinal_bound = solver.best_objective_bound  # 最终 dual bound\nfinal_obj = solver.objective_value\n```\n\n参考：[CP-SAT Primer parameters](https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html)、[coding patterns](https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/06_coding_patterns.html)。\n\n## 9. Certificate 的形式化\n\nDRAT/LRAT 是 SAT 用的（[drat-trim](https://github.com/marijnheule/drat-trim)、[GRAT toolchain](https://www21.in.tum.de/~lammich/grat/)），优化问题用 [VeriPB](https://github.com/StephanGocht/VeriPB)。**CP-SAT 当前不输出任何形式化 proof**。可证 ε-optimal 比可证 OPTIMAL **严格更容易**——后者要 dual bound 完全收敛到 primal，前者只要 dual bound 进入 ε-tube。对我们项目：短期靠\"log + dual bound 三元组\"的非形式化 certificate 就够审计；如果未来要法务/合规级证明，再考虑把核心 ghost rect 部分转 PB 走 VeriPB。\n\n## 10. 风险与 lock 兼容性\n\nPROJECT_LOCK 当前 \"Forbidden Changes\" 没有显式禁止 ε-optimal，但 \"Exactness Constitution\" 写的是 `max_lex(area, min_side)`，没说允许 gap。所以走 ε 路线需要：(a) 新增 `epsilon_certified` 模式与 `certified_exact` 并列（**不取代**，符合 lock \"strictly separate paths\" 原则）；(b) 在 `rules/canonical_rules.json` 加 `gap_tolerance` 字段；(c) artifact schema 加 `dual_bound`/`relative_gap`/`gap_limit_hit` 三字段；(d) FILE_STATUS.md + spec + tests 联动。**升级路径**：先 5% gap 跑通整个 80x80 流程验证 pipeline，再渐进缩到 1%，最后无 gap 当 stretch goal。\n\n---\n\n## 我们项目接受 ε-optimal 的可能性 + 第一步具体方案\n\n**可能性评估**：高（80%）。理由：(1) 工业界基线本就是 1-5%；(2) 玩家对 area 1% 差异不可感知；(3) PROJECT_LOCK 只需新增模式而非破坏现有 `certified_exact`；(4) 168h 长跑已经隐含了\"时间预算限制\"，加 gap 限制是同一逻辑的延伸；(5) AI Safety Contract 不冲突（gap certificate 仍是数学产物，非 AI 推断）。\n\n**第一步具体方案（1 周内可落地）**：\n\n1. 在 `src/models/master_model.py` 加 `gap_tolerance: Optional[float] = None` 参数，传给 `solver.parameters.relative_gap_limit`\n2. 新建 `src/search/gap_certificate.py`：定义 `GapCertificate` dataclass = `(best_obj, best_bound, relative_gap, absolute_gap, wall_time, status, gap_limit_hit, time_series)`\n3. 在 `exact_campaign.py` checkpoint schema 加 `gap_certificate` 字段（向后兼容，老 checkpoint 该字段为 None 视为 0% gap）\n4. PROJECT_LOCK.md 加 \"Path C: epsilon_certified\" 一节，明确 `relative_gap ≤ 0.05` 的允许语义和 artifact 标记规则\n5. 跑一次 70x70 with `gap_tolerance=0.05`，对比基线 wall time、最终 area/min_side 差异，写一份 baseline 报告\n6. 如效果好（预期 wall time 降至 1/10~1/100），扩到 80x80 1.2 版本测试\n\n不需要立刻动 VeriPB 形式化证明——那是 \"phase 5 stretch\"，现阶段 dual bound 三元组足够。"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1382,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4394,"cache_read_input_tokens":44842,"output_tokens":3726,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4394},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1382,"output_tokens":3726,"cache_read_input_tokens":44842,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4394,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4394,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorfxnHaVyGPzG9Nz2Ev","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d5e82da6-c57d-4e5b-9c98-735e202f0747","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:53.475Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
